{%- comment -%}
  Full-screen predictive search overlay.

  Rendered once, from the header section, so there is exactly one overlay per
  page and it sits next to the control that opens it.

  Design: project/Nessclusive Storefront.dc.html:777-795.

  Progressive enhancement, in three parts:

  1. The header control stays `<a href="/search">`. ness-search.js intercepts a
     plain left-click on it; a modified click, a blocked script, or no script
     at all leaves a link that navigates to a real page.
  2. This element ships with `hidden` set. Without the script it never opens —
     which is correct, because everything inside it is reachable at /search.
     Nothing here is content that exists only in the overlay.
  3. The form inside is a real GET form with a real action. Enter submits it
     natively, so a shopper reaches full results even when the suggestion
     fetch is failing.

  The suggestions themselves are markup, not JSON: ness-search.js asks the
  Predictive Search API to render sections/ness-predictive-search.liquid and
  swaps the result in.

  No settings. Per PLAN.md's Editability policy the search overlay is entirely
  locked — there is nothing here a merchant could usefully change that would
  not also let them break it.
{%- endcomment -%}

{{ 'ness-search.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'ness-search.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

<ness-search id="ness-search" hidden>
  {%- comment -%}
    The scrim is a button so it is a real, described control rather than a
    click-handling div — but it is taken out of the tab order and the
    accessibility tree, because Escape and the close button are how a keyboard
    or screen reader user is meant to leave.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <button
    type="button"
    class="ness-search__scrim"
    data-search-scrim
    tabindex="-1"
    aria-hidden="true"
  ></button>

  <div
    class="ness-search__panel"
    data-search-panel
    role="dialog"
    aria-modal="true"
    aria-labelledby="ness-search-title"
  >
    <div class="ness-search__inner">
      <h2 id="ness-search-title" class="visually-hidden">Search</h2>

      <form
        class="ness-search__form"
        action="{{ routes.search_url }}"
        method="get"
        role="search"
      >
        {%- comment -%}
          Products only, matching what the overlay shows. `options[prefix]=last`
          makes the last word match on a prefix, so "clos" already finds
          "closure" — the behaviour a shopper expects while still typing.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <input type="hidden" name="type" value="product">
        <input type="hidden" name="options[prefix]" value="last">

        <svg
          class="ness-search__icon"
          width="22"
          height="22"
          viewBox="0 0 16 16"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <circle cx="6.8" cy="6.8" r="6.05"/>
          <path d="M11.4 11.4 L15.2 15.2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        </svg>

        <label class="visually-hidden" for="ness-search-input">Search products</label>
        <input
          id="ness-search-input"
          class="ness-search__input"
          data-search-input
          type="search"
          name="q"
          placeholder="Search wigs, closures, accessories"
          autocomplete="off"
          autocorrect="off"
          autocapitalize="off"
          spellcheck="false"
        >

        {%- comment -%}
          A single text field submits on Enter without this button, and the
          design shows no submit control — but that leaves screen reader and
          switch users with no named way to run the search. Visually hidden
          keeps the design exact and the control real.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <button type="submit" class="visually-hidden">Search</button>

        <button
          type="button"
          class="ness-search__close"
          data-search-close
          aria-label="Close search"
        >
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M1 1L15 15M15 1L1 15" stroke-linecap="round"/>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </form>

      {%- comment -%}
        The summary is both the design's result line and the live region that
        tells a screen reader how many results arrived — the count is otherwise
        conveyed only by a grid appearing silently below the field.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p
        class="ness-search__summary"
        data-search-summary
        role="status"
        aria-live="polite"
        aria-atomic="true"
      ></p>

      {%- comment -%}
        Deliberately unlabelled and role-less: an aria-label on a plain div is
        ignored, and giving it a landmark role would add a second navigable
        region inside a modal that already has one accessible name. The
        summary above states what the grid contains.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="ness-search__results" data-search-results></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</ness-search>
